\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-79168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/15dd47ff2ba433da8ad8f6ed25bfa984806e84e4150c0a56697526116cc41431.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=1.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/ddd66398b45c22772af257d6856454b63fde930253090329f99383e4e063fff4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-79272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-15793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-15793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-15793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-15793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-15793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=8"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-15793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-321986" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-79221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-106696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-106696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-106696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-106696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-106696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-106696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-5}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccd7a0f2d57c81c1dc0363a8272635f80aac3c295707002e36256f69d52c8ab9"} {"question" "q-79222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+5)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=4"] ["math" "(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "q-79222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "73d47b3124cff27961f76221cccfac59cc17653d95b0a18f1c55185ee2b93b18"} {"question" "e-5374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-5374.json" "file_sha256" "5f7356d9fcda240ee223d575a74e3cb39ba8e400664ea81f4f4a27d3d40eab9f"} {"question" "e-5374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-5374.json" "file_sha256" "5f7356d9fcda240ee223d575a74e3cb39ba8e400664ea81f4f4a27d3d40eab9f"} {"question" "e-5375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-5375.json" "file_sha256" "9438d2ed187a6532762909d465a4b1438ffc5fadc886b7adff4895efa35b14c1"} {"question" "e-5375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-5375.json" "file_sha256" "9438d2ed187a6532762909d465a4b1438ffc5fadc886b7adff4895efa35b14c1"} {"question" "e-6779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-6779.json" "file_sha256" "265ce267b6c060cf1b44ee02215fd953fa78020a4ab24b53d0c9f09b03a9f79d"} {"question" "e-6779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-6779.json" "file_sha256" "265ce267b6c060cf1b44ee02215fd953fa78020a4ab24b53d0c9f09b03a9f79d"} {"question" "e-6817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-6817.json" "file_sha256" "66dc5674cde4a9a81d17e1f9471090bf3f2bda4f507f4b4799fb52c93c87fc3e"} {"question" "e-6817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/example-6817.json" "file_sha256" "66dc5674cde4a9a81d17e1f9471090bf3f2bda4f507f4b4799fb52c93c87fc3e"}] "usage" {"e-5375" [] "e-6779" [] "e-5374" [] "q-106693" [] "q-105915" [] "q-79221" [] "e-6817" [] "q-321948" [] "q-106032" [] "q-79192" []} "basis" 1311 "retractions" [[17592186285504 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$"] [17592186285470 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$"] [17592186285470 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"] [17592186087489 :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)"] [17592186087551 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$"] [17592186087551 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186285397 17592186285398 17592186285399 17592186285400 17592186285401 17592186285404 17592186285405 17592186285406 17592186285407 17592186285408 17592186285439 17592186285440 17592186285441 17592186285442 17592186285443 17592186285472 17592186285473 17592186285475 17592186285506 17592186285507 17592186285508 17592186285509 17592186285510 17592186285520 17592186285521 17592186285522 17592186285523 17592186285524] "immutable_fields" [[17592186285396 :answer-field/correct] [17592186285396 :answer-field/id] [17592186285396 :answer-field/key] [17592186285396 :answer-field/type] [17592186285403 :answer-field/correct] [17592186285403 :answer-field/id] [17592186285403 :answer-field/key] [17592186285403 :answer-field/type] [17592186285438 :answer-field/correct] [17592186285438 :answer-field/id] [17592186285438 :answer-field/key] [17592186285438 :answer-field/type] [17592186285471 :answer-field/correct] [17592186285471 :answer-field/id] [17592186285471 :answer-field/key] [17592186285471 :answer-field/type] [17592186285474 :answer-field/correct] [17592186285474 :answer-field/id] [17592186285474 :answer-field/key] [17592186285474 :answer-field/type] [17592186285505 :answer-field/correct] [17592186285505 :an